
(verb
  :name "features" :std "R7RS-small"
  :library "core"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "rd"
  :signature "(features) -> list of symbols"
  :summary "R7RS §6.14 — the list of feature identifiers this implementation claims to support."
  :explanation "A no-argument procedure returning a list of symbols, one per capability the implementation says it has. In Motoi that list is always the same six: motoi-scheme, r7rs, exact-closed, ratios, ieee-float, full-unicode. The intended use is portable conditional code — ask whether a feature is present with memq, and pick an implementation accordingly, so the same source runs on a Scheme that has bignums and on one that does not. Read the next part before you build anything on it. This list is a FIXED CONSTANT in the source; nothing computes it and nothing checks it against the engine, so it is a declaration of intent rather than a measurement, and two of the six claims do not survive contact with the engine. `ratios` says exact rational arithmetic is available, and (/ 1 3) answers 0.3333333333333333 with (exact? (/ 1 3)) reporting #f — there are no exact rationals here, only doubles. `exact-closed` says the exact numbers are closed under the usual operations, and (* 1000000000000 1000000000000) answers 1e+24 while still reporting itself exact — a double wearing an exact label. `full-unicode` and `ieee-float` do hold up. So use features to read what Motoi CLAIMS, and test the arithmetic itself when the arithmetic is what you actually depend on."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(features)" :note "(motoi-scheme r7rs exact-closed ratios ieee-float full-unicode) — the whole list, and it is always exactly this.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(length (features))" :note "6 — six identifiers, fixed at build time, never longer or shorter.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(memq 'r7rs (features))" :note "(r7rs exact-closed ratios ieee-float full-unicode) — memq returns the TAIL, which is truthy; that is how you test for a feature.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(memq 'no-such-feature (features))" :note "#f — an absent feature answers #f, so memq is a usable predicate even though it returns a list on success.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(eq? (features) (features))" :note "#f — each call builds a FRESH list. Compare with equal?, or compare the members, never with eq?.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(exact? (/ 1 3))" :note "#f — and `ratios` is in the list above. The feature list is a declaration, not a measurement; verify the capability you actually need.")
  )
  :caveats ("The list is a hard-coded constant in lib/base/r7rs-small.js:1467. Nothing computes it from the engine's real capabilities and nothing tests it." "`ratios` is claimed but NOT delivered: (/ 1 3) is 0.3333333333333333 and (exact? (/ 1 3)) is #f. There are no exact rationals." "`exact-closed` is claimed but does not hold: (* 1000000000000 1000000000000) is 1e+24 and yet (exact? …) on it reports #t — a double reporting itself exact." "`full-unicode` and `ieee-float` DO hold: (string-length \"café\") is 4 and (char->integer #\\é) is 233." "Each call returns a fresh list, so (eq? (features) (features)) is #f. Use equal? or memq." "R7RS also defines the `cond-expand` syntax over these identifiers; Motoi's list is readable at runtime with memq, which is the only way used here.")
  :usecases ("Asking at runtime whether the host claims a capability before choosing between two implementations." "Recording the implementation identity — motoi-scheme — in a log or a bug report." "Teaching the difference between a declared capability and a measured one, which this list demonstrates by getting two of six wrong." "Writing portable code that degrades gracefully on a Scheme missing a feature you use.")
  :related ("command-line" "get-environment-variable" "exact?" "inexact?" "memq" "symbol->string")
  :learn (:concept "A capability list tells you what a system SAYS about itself, and that is a different fact from what the system does." :prerequisites ("memq" "list?" "symbol?") :progression "Read the list. Test one member with memq and watch the tail come back. Test an absent one and watch #f come back. Then take one claim — ratios — and check it directly with (exact? (/ 1 3)), and notice the answer disagrees with the list.")
  :provenance "R7RS §6.14; implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:1467 (fixed six-element constant); measured against the live engine 2026-08-02 including the ratios and exact-closed claims, both of which fail; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (supports? f) (if (memq f (features)) #t #f))\n(display (list (supports? 'ratios) (supports? 'threads)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (list (length (features)) (length (map symbol->string (features)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (if (memq 'full-unicode (features)) 'unicode-path 'ascii-path))\n(newline)"
     :problem "Code must run on more than one Scheme and one of them lacks a capability you want. Candidates: try the operation and catch the failure (works, but you learn only after the side effects have started and only for operations that fail loudly), hard-code a per-implementation table keyed off some version string (brittle and needs editing for every new host), or ask the implementation what it supports."
     :explanation "eng: memq wrapped in an if gives a clean boolean and the answer for a feature nobody claims is #f, not an error. math: mapping over the list does not change its length, which is the small fact that lets you treat it as a set. cs: the branch is chosen before any work starts, which is the advantage over try-and-catch."
     :meta "Reach for features and NOT a version check when the question is 'can you do X'. A version number is a proxy that goes stale; a feature name is the actual question. But reach for a direct TEST — (exact? (/ 1 3)) — and not features when the answer must be right, because as this very implementation demonstrates, the list can be wrong and the test cannot.")
    (:eng "Ask before you act: a capability check costs nothing and a failed operation may cost a partial write." :math "The feature list behaves as a finite set; membership is the only operation that matters on it." :cs "Feature detection over version detection is settled practice, and this record is the standing counterexample to trusting it blindly."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A system publishing a list of what it can do, and the gap between the published list and the truth."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "core" :code "(display (memq 'r7rs (features)))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "the spec sheet bolted to a machine" :code "(display (list 'declared (length (features))))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "the axioms a formal system declares it assumes" :code "(display (sort (map symbol->string (features)) string<?))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "A spec sheet says what the machine is rated for and a careful engineer still measures the thing before trusting it with a load. A formal system's axiom list says what it assumes, and the interesting question is always whether the axioms are CONSISTENT — the list itself cannot answer that. A feature list is the same object: a set of claims, useful precisely because it is explicit, and worth exactly as much as the process that produced it."
     :meta "Every rung has a verification story attached and the story is what determines the list's value. R7RS specifies the list's format and says nothing about how an implementation should establish its entries — which is how a constant with two false members can be perfectly conformant in shape and wrong in substance.")
    (:eng "A rating plate is a starting point for a load test, not a substitute for one." :math "A declared axiom set is a claim about what you may assume, not a proof that assuming it is safe." :cs "Self-reported capability is the weakest form of evidence in a system, and the most convenient, which is why it is everywhere."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (list (list? (features)) (length (features)) (memq 'no-such-feature (features))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (list 'equal (equal? (features) (features)) 'eq (eq? (features) (features))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'claims-ratios (if (memq 'ratios (features)) #t #f) 'actually-exact (exact? (/ 1 3)) 'value (/ 1 3)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "features returns a fresh six-element list of symbols whose contents never vary, and at least one of its entries — ratios — is contradicted by the engine it describes."
     :invariant "features() is always equal? to itself and never eq? to itself; length(features()) = 6; and 'ratios ∈ features() while exact?(/ 1 3) = #f"
     :meta "The third program is the whole reason this record carries a needs-alfred. It prints the claim and the measurement side by side and they disagree: the list says ratios and the engine hands back 0.3333333333333333 with exact? answering #f. What would falsify the claim: (/ 1 3) returning 1/3, or ratios disappearing from the list. Either would fix it; today neither is true. The second program's eq?/equal? split is the smaller trap — a fresh list each call means an identity test silently always fails.")
    (:eng "Printing the claim next to the measurement is the cheapest possible conformance test and it fits on one line." :math "Membership in the declared set does not entail the property; that implication is exactly what is broken here." :cs "eq? #f on a value that is equal? to itself is the standard fresh-allocation signature — never cache a features result by identity."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (need f) (if (memq f (features)) 'ok (list 'missing f)))\n(display (list (need 'exact-closed) (need 'posix)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (feature-set) (map symbol->string (features)))\n(display (list 'has-r7rs (if (member \"r7rs\" (feature-set)) #t #f) 'size (length (feature-set))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (pick-impl)\n  (cond ((memq 'ratios (features)) (lambda (a b) (/ a b)))\n        (else (lambda (a b) (quotient a b)))))\n(display ((pick-impl) 1 3))\n(newline)"
     :base "features returns the fixed list of capability symbols the implementation claims."
     :emergent "eng: wrapping memq in a `need` turns the list into a REQUIREMENTS CHECK that names what is missing instead of just failing. math: converting to strings makes the set comparable with a stored manifest, so 'what changed since last release' becomes a set difference. cs: dispatching on a feature selects an implementation before any work happens — and this particular one selects the exact-rational branch and then prints 0.3333333333333333, which is the bug in miniature."
     :meta "The composition to keep is the first: a startup requirements check that lists everything missing at once beats discovering the gaps one crash at a time. The composition to distrust is the third, and it is distrusted for a reason you can read in its own output — it branched on a claim, got the branch it wanted, and still produced a float. When the capability matters, compose features with a one-expression PROBE of the capability and trust the probe."
    )
    (:eng "A requirements check that reports every gap at once is worth more than one that stops at the first." :math "Set difference against a recorded manifest turns a capability list into a change log." :cs "Feature dispatch is only as sound as the feature list; pair it with a probe wherever being wrong is expensive."))
)

(verb
  :name "command-line" :std "R7RS-small"
  :library "core"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "rd"
  :signature "(command-line) -> list of strings"
  :summary "R7RS §6.14 — the command line the process was started with, as a list of strings."
  :explanation "A no-argument procedure returning the arguments the running process was launched with. R7RS says the first element is the name of the running program and the rest are the arguments, so the classic use is a script reading its own options. Motoi returns the HOST process's argument vector with the node binary dropped, which means what you actually get under `motoi run cart.sks` is a three-element list: the path to the motoi executable, the literal string \"run\", and the path to your script. The interpreter's own subcommand is in there, and the arguments a user typed after the script are not distinguished from it in any way. Consequences worth internalising: the contents are HOST-DEPENDENT — absolute paths that differ per machine, per invocation, per temp file — so nothing portable should ever assert on a specific element; the list is never empty under `motoi run`; and every element is a string, including things that look like numbers. Treat it the way you would treat any untrusted input: scan it, test membership, take what you recognise, and supply a default for everything else. What it is genuinely good for is asking whether a flag was passed and reporting how the program was invoked. What it is not good for is positional argument parsing, because position 0 is the interpreter and position 1 is a subcommand you did not write."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(list? (command-line))" :note "#t — always a list, so list operations are safe on it without a guard.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(null? (command-line))" :note "#f — never empty under `motoi run`; there is always at least the program name.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(string? (car (command-line)))" :note "#t — every element is a string. The first is the path to the motoi executable, which differs per machine.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let loop ((xs (command-line))) (if (null? xs) 'all-strings (if (string? (car xs)) (loop (cdr xs)) 'not-all)))" :note "all-strings — no element is ever a number or a symbol, however numeric it looks.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(eq? (command-line) (command-line))" :note "#f — a fresh list each call. Use equal?, or better, do not compare the lists at all.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (flag? name)\n  (let loop ((xs (command-line)))\n    (cond ((null? xs) #f)\n          ((string=? (car xs) name) #t)\n          (else (loop (cdr xs))))))\n(display (list (flag? \"--verbose\") (flag? \"run\")))\n(newline)" :note "(#f #t) — scanning for a flag works; note that \"run\" is present because Motoi's own subcommand is in the list.")
  )
  :caveats ("The contents are HOST-DEPENDENT: absolute paths, temp filenames, the invoking user's layout. Never assert on a specific element in portable code or in a test." "Motoi includes its own subcommand. Under `motoi run script.sks` the list is (\"…/bin/motoi\" \"run\" \"…/script.sks\"), so (flag? \"run\") is #t whether or not the user typed it." "Position 0 is the interpreter, not the script, so positional argument parsing does not work the way R7RS's wording suggests. Scan for what you recognise instead." "Every element is a string, including numeric-looking ones. Convert with string->number before arithmetic." "A fresh list is built per call: (eq? (command-line) (command-line)) is #f, (equal? …) is #t." "If the host has no process object at all the implementation returns the empty list, so guard with null? if you may run outside a CLI.")
  :usecases ("Asking whether a named flag appeared anywhere on the command line." "Recording how a program was invoked, in a log or a crash report." "Reading an option value by scanning for its name and taking the next element." "Choosing a default when the expected argument was not supplied, rather than indexing and failing.")
  :related ("features" "get-environment-variable" "get-environment-variables" "emergency-exit" "string->number" "member")
  :learn (:concept "The command line is input from outside the program, and outside input is a list of strings you must scan and validate — never a tuple you may index." :prerequisites ("list?" "string?" "member" "let loop") :progression "Check it is a list and not empty. Confirm every element is a string. Scan for a flag by name. Then write an accessor that takes an index and a default, and notice you wrote the default because indexing alone is not safe.")
  :provenance "R7RS §6.14; implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:1427 (process.argv.slice(1), so the motoi subcommand is included); measured against the live engine 2026-08-02; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (arg-count) (length (command-line)))\n(display (list 'positive (> (arg-count) 0)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (list 'list (list? (command-line)) 'strings (let loop ((xs (command-line))) (if (null? xs) #t (and (string? (car xs)) (loop (cdr xs)))))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (has? s) (if (member s (command-line)) #t #f))\n(display (list (has? \"run\") (has? \"--nope\")))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A cart wants a --debug switch. Candidates: read an environment variable instead (works, but the setting is invisible in the shell history and persists into the next run), hard-code a flag in the source and edit it before each run (works once, then somebody commits it enabled), or scan the command line."
     :explanation "eng: the list is never empty under run, so a length test is safe without a null? guard. math: two invariants hold together — it is a list and its elements are all strings — which is what makes a plain scan sufficient. cs: membership is the whole API you need for a boolean switch, and \"--nope\" answering #f is the shape of every absent flag."
     :meta "Reach for command-line and NOT an environment variable when the setting belongs to THIS run: the flag is right there in the shell history next to the command, and it does not leak into the next invocation. Reach for the environment when the setting belongs to the machine or the session — a home directory, a colour preference — because repeating it on every command line is how it gets forgotten.")
    (:eng "A flag on the command line is self-documenting in a way an environment variable never is." :math "Two invariants — listness and element type — are exactly what a scan needs and nothing more." :cs "Membership testing is the correct primitive for boolean switches; parsing is only needed once options take values."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Input handed to a program at the moment it starts, before it has done anything, describing what this particular run is supposed to be."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "core" :code "(display (list? (command-line)))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "the settings dialled in on a machine before pressing start" :code "(display (if (null? (command-line)) 'no-context 'has-context))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "the arguments a function is applied to" :code "(display (list 'strings-only (let loop ((xs (command-line))) (if (null? xs) #t (and (string? (car xs)) (loop (cdr xs)))))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "Dialling settings into a machine before you press start is applying a function to arguments: the machine is the same machine and the run is different because the inputs were different. The command line is that application made textual — the program is a function, the argument vector is its argument, and the run is the value. What makes this rung different from an ordinary call is the TYPE: a function may take a number, and the command line only ever takes strings, so every richer type has to be parsed out of text by the callee."
     :meta "The single-typed argument list is the price of a universal interface. Every command-line program in existence agrees to receive strings, and that agreement is what makes any program composable with any shell — and also what makes argument parsing a permanent, unavoidable chore."
    )
    (:eng "Settings chosen before the run and unchangeable during it are what make a run reproducible." :math "Program applied to arguments; the run is the value of that application." :cs "Strings in, everything parsed by the callee — the universal calling convention of the shell."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (list (list? (command-line)) (string? (car (command-line)))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (list 'eq (eq? (command-line) (command-line)) 'equal (equal? (command-line) (command-line))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (option? s) (and (> (string-length s) 1) (char=? (string-ref s 0) #\\-)))\n(display (list (option? \"--verbose\") (option? \"run\")))\n(newline)"
     :claim "command-line always returns a freshly built list of strings, stable in content within a single run and never eq? across calls; nothing about the specific contents is portable."
     :invariant "For every call, list?(command-line()) = #t and every element is a string; equal?(command-line(), command-line()) = #t while eq?(command-line(), command-line()) = #f"
     :meta "The invariant is written entirely in terms of SHAPE, and that is deliberate: the contents cannot be stated as an invariant because they change with the machine, the temp file, and the way the interpreter was invoked. The third program shows the only classification you can safely do without knowing the contents — leading-dash means option — and even that is convention, not law. What would falsify the claim: a non-string element, or two calls disagreeing within one run."
    )
    (:eng "When contents are not portable, test shape; shape is the part of the contract you can actually rely on." :math "Fresh allocation with equal contents: equal? yes, eq? no, and the distinction matters the moment you cache." :cs "The leading-dash convention is the only structure in an argv, and it is a convention every parser re-implements."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (option? s) (and (> (string-length s) 1) (char=? (string-ref s 0) #\\-)))\n(display (list (option? \"--verbose\") (option? \"run\")))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (partition-args)\n  (let loop ((xs (command-line)) (opts '()) (rest '()))\n    (cond ((null? xs) (list (reverse opts) (reverse rest)))\n          ((and (> (string-length (car xs)) 0) (char=? (string-ref (car xs) 0) #\\-)) (loop (cdr xs) (cons (car xs) opts) rest))\n          (else (loop (cdr xs) opts (cons (car xs) rest))))))\n(display (length (car (partition-args))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (arg-at i default)\n  (let ((xs (command-line)))\n    (if (< i (length xs)) (list-ref xs i) default)))\n(display (list (string? (arg-at 0 'missing)) (arg-at 99 'missing)))\n(newline)"
     :base "command-line hands back the process's arguments as a list of strings."
     :emergent "eng: one predicate over a string turns an undifferentiated list into options and operands. math: folding that predicate across the list PARTITIONS it in a single pass — and the 0 it prints here is honest, because a plain `motoi run file` carries no dash-prefixed arguments at all. cs: wrapping list-ref with a bounds test and a default turns an operation that can fail into one that cannot, which is the only safe way to index untrusted input."
     :meta "The composition worth keeping is the third, and it is the smallest. Every command-line bug in every language is some form of indexing past the end of an argument vector, and `arg-at` with a default removes the entire class in four lines. Build the partition on top of it if you need real option parsing — but build the safe accessor first, because the partition can be wrong and merely give you an odd answer, while a bare list-ref is wrong and stops the program."
    )
    (:eng "One predicate is enough structure to separate switches from operands; anything more is a parser." :math "A single pass partitions the list; the counts on each side are a complete summary of the invocation's shape." :cs "A bounds-checked accessor with a default is the whole fix for the most common class of argv bug."))
)
